  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nEarly Sunday afternoon temperatures are warming into the 70s under \nmostly sunny skies for another excellent Autumn day. There isn't \nmuch change over the next several days as the forecast more or less \nstays the same. Winds will be generally light and variable over the \nnext few days under the high pressure. One consideration is that we \nwill have efficient mixing that will support RH's to fall into the \n25 to 35 percent range. Fortunately for us, fire weather concerns \nwill be limited given the light/variable winds in this weather \npattern. \n\nOur attention turns to the end of the week into next weekend. Our \nupper level ridge will begin to flatten and lose influence over the \neastern US. Upstream, a series of weak shortwaves will move through \nthe Great Lakes region and allow moisture to return to increase. \nThis trend of an earlier weakening of the ridge & surface high \npressure will lead to a chance of isolated showers Saturday evening. \nThis weekend will be split Sunday will be the Trick and Saturday \nshould be the treat. Given the residual low level dry air in place, \nHalloween will not be a wash out. Worst case scenario will be a few \nsprinkles or very isolated showers across portions of the valley. In \nfact, the overcast skies will help set the mood for a great night of \ntrick or treating! \n\nSunday, the trick, will be a rather crummy day as the slug of \nmoisture tied to the system moves into the region. There is an \nincreasing signal for a rather widespread rain event Sunday across \nguidance. It doesn't look like a ton of rain, but it should be \nenough for everyone to get a tenth or two as the pattern begins to \ntransition to a more active look into early November.",
